  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday...\n\nAreas of fog and low clouds persist across much of the north-\ncentral MT plains, where a very moist low level airmass is in \nplace on the southwestern fridge of surface high pressure centered\nacross the Canadian Prairies. Light low level easterly upslope \nflow is supporting some of the most widespread/dense fog affecting\nareas adjacent to the Rocky Mountain Front and Cascade county. A \nDense Fog Advisory continues through 21z adjacent to the Rocky \nMtn Front. \n\nTonight through Friday night...\n\nSurface high pressure currently centered across southern AB/SK \nslowly slides eastward through Friday night and is likely to hold \nthe shallow/moist airmass in place across the the plains, \nmaintaining at least some areas of low clouds and fog across \nportions of north-central MT. Dense fog advisories will likely be \nneeded again tonight through Friday morning for many of the same \nlocations experiencing fog today. Latest high resolution model \nensembles give a 60% probability for visibility 1/2 mile or less \nfor areas of north-central MT along and west of I-15. \nAdditionally, overnight temperatures near or slightly below \nfreezing will support freezing fog again tonight as well, and even\nthe potential for some patchy freezing drizzle with the risk for \nlocalized icing, particularly affecting bridges and overpasses. \n\nThis weekend...\nAn upper level low over the Pacific Northwest will dig southeast \ntowards Utah into Saturday. This will result in a moist southerly\nflow aloft to developing  over Central MT. As a result, rain/snow\nis forecat to develop Saturday night over Southwest MT and then \nmove into Central MT by Sunday morning. Latest model guidance \ncontinues to support widespread precipitation development Saturday\nnight into Sunday with precipitation exiting the area Sunday \nnight or Monday morning. This storm system will bring rain/snow \nto much of the CWA. Snow levels will be high to start, generally \nabove 7000 feet on Saturday, but then are forecast to fall down to\nabout 4000 feet Monday. By Monday, most of the precipitation will\nhave exited the region, but mountain snowfall is likely Saturday \nnight into Sunday. Snow accumulations around the Pony/Big Sky area\nare starting to approach warning criteria. Thus a winter storm \nwatch might be needed for Zone 330. Otherwise, a winter weather \nadvisory is looking likely for mountain areas of Southwest and \nportions of Central MT from Saturday night into Sunday night.\n\nAs the next upper level ridge starts to build over the northern \nRockies. The main question is how strong will it be. Overall, we \nexpect drier and slightly warmer conditions by the later portions\nof next week as this upper level expands. Afternoon temperatures \nmight be a bit warmer than currently forecast for the later half \nof next week. Hoenisch/Brusda/WMR",
